I've gathered sufficient evidence and recorded three well-separated traces for the miniFE repository, reflecting the three clearly distinct build/parallelism variants found in the source tree:

## Summary

**Subject**: miniFE (Mantevo mini-application) — an unstructured implicit finite-element proxy app whose core kernel is a Conjugate Gradient (CG) sparse-linear-solver loop (matvec, waxpby, dot) over a 3D structured decomposition.

**Variants recorded** (each with its own trace, since they have materially different scheduling shapes):

1. **`miniFE:mpi`** (ref/ directory) — pure MPI, process parallelism only. Halo exchange (Isend/Irecv/Wait over neighbor list) each matvec + MPI_Allreduce per CG iteration for dot products. Classic sparse-CG {neighbor, reduction} communication set. No threading, no GPU, no parallel I/O, no checkpointing.

2. **`miniFE:mpi-openmp`** (openmp/, openmp-opt/, openmp-opt-knl/, openmp45/ directories) — MPI + OpenMP hybrid. Same halo/reduction communication skeleton as ref, but Vector_functions and SparseMatrix kernels are parallelized with `#pragma omp parallel for` / `reduction(+:...)`, with `#pragma omp atomic` for scatter-add during FE assembly.

3. **`miniFE:mpi-cuda`** (cuda/ directory) — MPI + CUDA, one GPU per rank. ELL sparse matvec run as CUDA kernels (`matvec_ell_kernel<<<>>>`), CUDA streams/events used to overlap the interior matvec with the MPI halo exchange (`MATVEC_OVERLAP`), and an optional `GPUDIRECT` build flag that routes MPI messages directly to/from device pointers — i.e., a gpu-aware MPI communication path.

All three share the same underlying algorithmic communication pattern (structured-grid neighbor halo exchange + small global reductions per CG iteration), which is why `communication.pattern={neighbor,reduction}` and `communication.latency_sensitive=true` appear in all traces, while the differentiators (parallelism set, accelerator fields, gpu_comm) separate them correctly as required.

I did not create separate traces for kokkos/, qthreads/, cilk/, mkl/, nvamg/ since these are either thin recompiles of the same MPI+threads(+CUDA) skeleton with a different node abstraction (Kokkos/TBB/TPI/Qthreads/Cilk backends selecting between the same CPU-parallel-for or CUDA-kernel code paths already captured above) or a substantially different solver (nvamg uses AMG/FGMRES config files, not the CG miniFE kernel) that would need deeper investigation beyond the requested scope; the three recorded variants cover the distinct, evidenced scheduling shapes.
